2025-08-03 16:17:58 [nitro@ryzen5] what was playstation 1 mips serial port ?
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] The PlayStation 1's serial port used the MIPS R3000A CPU's built-in SIO (Serial
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] I/O) interface, which was primarily used for:
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] Key Features:
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] - Baud Rate: Programmable (typically up to 115.2 kbps)
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] - Data Format: 8-bit, no parity, 1 stop bit (8N1 by default)
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] - Registers: Memory-mapped (accessed via `0x1F80xxxx` in the PS1's memory map)
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] - Usage:
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat]   - Debugging (early devkits)
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat]   - Loading code via serial (Net Yaroze)
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat]   - Peripheral communication (rarely used in retail games)
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] Register Access (Similar to Your Macros):
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] The PS1's SIO was controlled via registers like:
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] - Status/Control Registers (for TX/RX ready flags, interrupts)
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] - Data Register (for sending/receiving bytes)
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] Example (Simplified):
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] define SIO_BASE 0x1F801058  // Base address for SIO
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] void sio_putc(char c) {
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat]     while (!(*(volatile uint32_t*)(SIO_BASE + 0x14) & 0x2)); // Wait for TX ready
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat]     *(volatile uint32_t*)(SIO_BASE + 0x18) = c; // Send byte
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] Net Yaroze & Development:
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] - Net Yaroze (homebrew devkit) used the serial port for uploading code.
2025-08-03 16:18:15 [deepseek-chat] - Official devkits used it for debugging before moving to parallel port-based solutions.
