2025-08-04 18:20:20 [nitro@ryzen5] is this correct decoding?
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] Good catch! The jump instruction decoding in the disassembler has a potential issue. Let me analyze
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] it:
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] Current code:
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] case 'M': // j,jal
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat]   output+=hex(((loc&0xf0000000)|(op&0x03ffffff))<<2);
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat]   break;
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] The calculation `(loc&0xf0000000)|(op&0x03ffffff))<<2` combines:
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] 1. Upper 4 bits of current PC (`loc`)
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] 2. 26-bit immediate from instruction (`op`)
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] 3. Left-shifts by 2 (since MIPS jumps use word addresses)
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] This is mostly correct for MIPS, but there are two considerations:
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] 1. The shift should happen before the OR operation to properly align the immediate field:
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat]    ((loc & 0xf0000000) | ((op & 0x03ffffff) << 2))
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] 2. For absolute correctness, you might want to mask the final address to 32 bits since JavaScript
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] uses 64-bit numbers:
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat]    ((loc & 0xf0000000) | ((op & 0x03ffffff) << 2)) & 0xffffffff
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] So the corrected version would be:
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] case 'M': // j,jal
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat]   output += hex(((loc & 0xf0000000) | ((op & 0x03ffffff) << 2)) & 0xffffffff);
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat]   break;
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] This properly handles the MIPS jump instruction encoding where:
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] - The target address is formed by concatenating the upper 4 bits of PC
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] - With the 26-bit immediate field shifted left 2 bits
2025-08-04 18:20:45 [deepseek-chat] - Resulting in a 32-bit word-aligned address
2025-08-04 18:21:09 [nitro@ryzen5] i think this
