2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] Yes, your version is correct for standard MIPS jump instruction decoding:
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] output += hex((loc & 0xf0000000) | ((op & 0x03ffffff) << 2);
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] This properly:
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] 1. Takes the upper 4 bits of current PC (`loc & 0xf0000000`)
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] 2. Combines with the 26-bit target address (`op & 0x03ffffff`) shifted left 2 bits (for word
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] alignment)
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] 3. Results in a 32-bit jump target address
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] The bitwise OR operation will naturally produce a 32-bit result in JavaScript (since all numbers
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] are treated as 32-bit for bitwise operations), so no additional masking is needed.
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] This matches the MIPS architecture specification where:
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] - J-type instructions use 26-bit immediate fields
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] - The target address is PC[31:28] || (imm26 << 2)
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] - Effectively creating a 28-bit absolute address within the same 256MB region
2025-08-04 18:21:26 [deepseek-chat] Your implementation is correct and more concise than my previous suggestion.
