2025-08-05 13:23:31 [deepseek-chat] Yes, you can include debug symbols in the assembly build by adding the `--gstabs` or `--gdwarf2`
2025-08-05 13:23:31 [deepseek-chat] flag to the assembler. Here's the modified command:
2025-08-05 13:23:31 [deepseek-chat] mipsel-elf-as -msoft-float --gstabs -o bios.o bios.s
2025-08-05 13:23:31 [deepseek-chat] Or for DWARF debug info (more modern):
2025-08-05 13:23:31 [deepseek-chat] mipsel-elf-as -msoft-float --gdwarf2 -o bios.o bios.s
2025-08-05 13:23:31 [deepseek-chat] This will generate debug symbols that can be used with GDB or other debuggers. Make sure to also
2025-08-05 13:23:31 [deepseek-chat] include `-g` in your linker flags for complete debug information.
2025-08-05 13:24:10 [nitro@ryzen5] which is simpler for me to support in my emulator?
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] For your emulator, `--gstabs` (STABS format) is generally simpler to support than DWARF because:
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] 1. STABS has a more straightforward structure
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] 2. It's easier to parse basic symbol information
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] 3. DWARF has more complex features (variable tracking, CFI, etc.) that you likely don't need
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] Use this for simplest support:
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] mipsel-elf-as -msoft-float --gstabs -o bios.o bios.s
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] STABS will give you:
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] - Basic symbol names
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] - Source line number mappings
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] - Function boundaries
2025-08-05 13:24:20 [deepseek-chat] DWARF is more powerful but overkill unless you need advanced debugging features.
2025-08-05 13:25:16 [nitro@ryzen5] can i change this to use linker directly or is it best to use gcc ?
2025-08-05 13:25:18 [nitro@ryzen5] mipsel-elf-gcc -msoft-float -nostdlib -Ttext=0x80010000 -o mips1.elf bios.o mipsrom.o
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] For your case, using `mipsel-elf-gcc` as the linker driver is actually the better approach because:
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] 1. It automatically handles:
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat]    - C library startup code (even with `-nostdlib`)
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat]    - Passing the correct paths to the real linker (`mipsel-elf-ld`)
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat]    - Architecture-specific linking requirements
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] 2. The equivalent direct linker command would be more verbose:
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] mipsel-elf-ld -Ttext=0x80010000 -o mips1.elf bios.o mipsrom.o
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] But you might need to add:
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - Entry point (`-e _start`)
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - Additional library paths
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - Explicit linker script for more complex cases
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] Stick with `mipsel-elf-gcc` unless you need fine-grained linker control. The GCC frontend
2025-08-05 13:25:29 [deepseek-chat] simplifies the process while still giving you full control via `-nostdlib`.
2025-08-05 13:25:55 [nitro@ryzen5] are symbols retained?
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] Yes, symbols will be retained in the final ELF executable if you:
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] 1. Built objects with debug info (`-g` for GCC, `--gstabs` for ASM)
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] 2. Didn't strip them (`-s` or `--strip-all` not used)
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] Your current command:
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] mipsel-elf-gcc -msoft-float -nostdlib -Ttext=0x80010000 -o mips1.elf bios.o mipsrom.o
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] • will preserve symbols* from:
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] - `bios.o` (if built with `--gstabs`)
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] - `mipsrom.o` (if built with `-g`)
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] To verify symbols are present:
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] mipsel-elf-nm mips1.elf        # Show symbols
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] mipsel-elf-readelf -s mips1.elf # Detailed symbol table
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] If you need to strip debug symbols later:
2025-08-05 13:26:09 [deepseek-chat] mipsel-elf-strip --strip-debug mips1.elf  # Keep only essential symbols
