2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] The second column letters in `nm` output indicate the symbol type:
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - T - Text (code) section symbol (executable instructions)
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - t - Same as T but local (not visible outside object)
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - B - BSS section symbol (uninitialized data)
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - b - Same as B but local
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - R - Read-only data section symbol
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - D - Initialized data section symbol
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - U - Undefined (needed but not found in this object)
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - C - Common symbol (uninitialized, may merge with others)
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] In your output:
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - `T _start` = Global text symbol (entry point)
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - `B heap` = Global uninitialized variable
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - `b _gp` = Local uninitialized variable
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] - `R _fdata` = Global read-only data
2025-08-05 13:28:11 [deepseek-chat] The `Z` prefixes are C++ name mangling (like `Z9mallocmemi` = `mallocmem(int)`).
