2025-08-05 20:06:25 [nitro@ryzen5] what computer cpu used to render original toy story?
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] The original Toy Story (1995) was rendered primarily on Sun SPARCstation workstations,
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] which used SPARC (Scalable Processor Architecture) CPUs.
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] Pixar's render farm at the time consisted of hundreds of Sun SPARCstations running various SPARC
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] processors - likely SPARC v7 and early SPARC v8 chips running at speeds around 40-150 MHz. The
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] SPARC architecture was RISC-based, similar to MIPS in many ways.
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] The rendering took about 800,000 machine hours total, distributed across their render farm. Each
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] frame took between 45 minutes to 30 hours to render depending on complexity.
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] Sun workstations were the industry standard for high-end graphics work in the mid-90s, before x86
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] systems became powerful enough for serious 3D rendering. The SPARC architecture's clean RISC design
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] made it well-suited for the computational workloads of early CGI.
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] Interesting connection to your MIPS project - both SPARC and MIPS are clean RISC architectures from
2025-08-05 20:06:34 [claude-sonnet-4] that same era of computing!
