2025-08-06 09:31:21 [nitro@ryzen5] the messages can have a role but not a username?
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] That's correct! In the Anthropic API, messages have a `role` field but not a `username` field.
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] The standard message structure uses:
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] - `role`: either "user" or "assistant"
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] - `content`: the actual message content
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] So a typical message looks like:
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] {
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4]   "role": "user",
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4]   "content": "Hello, how are you?"
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] }
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] If you need to distinguish between different users or identify speakers, you'd typically include
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] that information within the content itself, like:
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] {
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4]   "role": "user",
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4]   "content": "John: How's the weather today?"
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] }
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] This is different from some other chat APIs that might have username or name fields. The Anthropic
2025-08-06 09:31:26 [claude-sonnet-4] API keeps it simple with just role and content.
