2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] Short answer: no — you usually do not need a new API key to A/B test different system prompts or
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] minor configuration changes.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] Details and when you might want separate keys
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] - Same key is fine for experimenting with different system messages, temperatures, model
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] parameters, or per-request instructions. System prompts are per-request, so you can send variant A
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] or B in the same account.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] - Use a separate key/account only when you need strict isolation (separate billing/quotas), avoid
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] rate‑limit interference, or separate production vs experiment environments.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] - If you test across different providers/accounts (OpenAI vs Anthropic vs Google) you will of
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] course need the relevant keys.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] Practical recommendations for reliable A/B tests
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 1. Keep variables controlled: change only the system prompt (or only temperature/model) between A
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] and B; keep everything else identical.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 2. Randomize assignment: randomly assign requests/users to A or B and record which variant was
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] used.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 3. Log everything: prompt variant, model/version, temperature, timestamp, request id, and the
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] response. Store outputs and metrics for later analysis.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 4. Tag requests: include a test-id or variant tag in metadata so you can filter logs.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 5. Use consistent model/version: change only one thing per experiment to attribute effects
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] correctly.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 6. Seed/Determinism: note that most LLMs are nondeterministic — use lower temperature or
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] deterministic options (if available) for clearer signal.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 7. Sample size & metrics: define success metric (e.g., helpfulness score, error rate) and collect
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] enough samples; apply appropriate statistical test.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 8. Rate limits & scale: if you expect heavy parallel traffic, a single key may hit rate
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] limits—consider extra keys/accounts to distribute load.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 9. Security: never hardcode keys; use environment variables and rotate keys if they get exposed.
2025-08-08 10:45:21 [gpt-5-mini] 10. Audit & rollback: keep experiment logs and a way to revert prompts/settings if issues appear.
