2025-08-09 11:07:54 [nitro@ryzen5] can you use pushd popd or is there a reason not to?
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] claude-sonnet-4-20250514: Absolutely! `pushd`/`popd` is actually the better approach here. There's
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] no reason not to use it, and it's cleaner than manually tracking the original directory.
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] • Improved version with pushd/popd:*
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] :analyse
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 	set "owner=%1"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 	set "repo=%2"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 	set "path=nitrologic\%repo%"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 	if exist "%path%" (
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		pushd "%path%"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		rem Get commit count
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		for /f %%i in ('git rev-list --count HEAD 2^>nul') do set "commits=%%i"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		if "%commits%"=="" set "commits=0"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		rem Get last commit date
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		for /f %%i in ('git log -1 --format^=%%cd --date^=short 2^>nul') do set "lastcommit=%%i"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		if "%lastcommit%"=="" set "lastcommit=none"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		rem Get file count
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		for /f %%i in ('git ls-files ^| find /c /v ""') do set "files=%%i"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		if "%files%"=="" set "files=0"
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		popd
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		echo %owner%,%repo%,yes,%commits%,%files%,%lastcommit% >> nitro_repos_stats.csv
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 	) else (
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 		echo %owner%,%repo%,no,0,0,none >> nitro_repos_stats.csv
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 	)
2025-08-09 11:08:01 [claude-sonnet-4] 	goto :eof
2025-08-09 11:08:02 [claude-sonnet-4] • Benefits of pushd/popd:*
2025-08-09 11:08:02 [claude-sonnet-4] - Automatically handles directory stack - no need to remember original path
2025-08-09 11:08:02 [claude-sonnet-4] - Works across drive boundaries (unlike `cd`)
2025-08-09 11:08:02 [claude-sonnet-4] - Cleaner, more robust code
2025-08-09 11:08:02 [claude-sonnet-4] - Less error-prone than manual path tracking
2025-08-09 11:08:02 [claude-sonnet-4] • The only potential downsides:*
2025-08-09 11:08:02 [claude-sonnet-4] - Directory stack has a limit (~32 levels typically)
2025-08-09 11:08:02 [claude-sonnet-4] - Very slightly more overhead than `cd`
2025-08-09 11:08:02 [claude-sonnet-4] But for your use case, `pushd`/`popd` is definitely the right choice!
