2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] Yes, you can call `git log` and count the lines of output in a `.bat` file. Here's a corrected
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] version of your script:
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] @echo off
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] setlocal enabledelayedexpansion
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] set log_count=0
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] if exist ".git" (
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat]     for /f "tokens=*" %%i in ('git log --oneline') do (
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat]         set /a log_count+=1
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat]     )
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat]     echo Number of commits: !log_count!
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] ) else (
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat]     echo .git directory not found.
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] )
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] Notes:
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] 1. `@echo off`: Disables command echoing for cleaner output.
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] 2. `setlocal enabledelayedexpansion`: Allows dynamic variable updates inside loops.
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] 3. `!logcount!`: Uses delayed expansion to access the updated value of `logcount`.
2025-08-09 12:20:50 [deepseek-chat] 4. Error Handling: Checks for the `.git` directory before proceeding.
