2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] A high commit count (like 2,943 for `nitrologic.github.io`) can happen for several reasons. Here
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] are the most common explanations:
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] 1. Frequent Small Commits
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    - If the repository is actively developed, small changes (e.g., typo fixes, minor updates) can
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] add up quickly.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    - Example: A blog or documentation site might have many small content updates.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] 2. Automated Commits
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    - Scripts or bots (e.g., CI/CD pipelines, content generators) may automatically commit changes.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    - Example: A static site generator rebuilding and committing files on every edit.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] 3. Rebasing or Rewriting History
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    - Force-pushing or rebasing can sometimes inflate commit counts artificially (though `rev-list`
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] counts the final history).
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] 4. Repository Age
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    - Older repositories naturally accumulate more commits over time.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] 5. Merge Commits
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    - If the repository uses a merge-heavy workflow (e.g., `git merge` instead of `rebase`), merge
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] commits can add to the count.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] How to Investigate:
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] 1. Check Commit History:
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    git log --oneline | wc -l
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    This should match the `rev-list --count` output.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] 2. Look for Patterns:
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    git log --since="1 year ago" --oneline | wc -l
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    See how many commits are recent.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] 3. Inspect Large Commits:
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    git log --stat -n 10
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    Check the nature of recent commits (e.g., tiny fixes vs. large changes).
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] 4. Blame the File:
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    git blame README.md
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat]    See who made frequent changes to a specific file.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] Next Steps:
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] - If the count seems unrealistic, you might want to audit the repository for automated or redundant
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] commits.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] - Tools like `git gc` can help clean up loose objects but won’t reduce the commit count.
2025-08-09 12:36:35 [deepseek-chat] Would you like help digging deeper into the commit history?
