2025-08-09 13:00:35 [nitro@ryzen5] in bat file I am using dir /s /-c , is there a way it can ignore dirs beginning with .?
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] Yes, you can exclude directories beginning with a dot (`.`) by using the `dir` command with the
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] `/AD` (directory attribute) and `/O-D` (sort by name) switches, combined with a `findstr` filter to
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] exclude the dot-prefixed directories. Here's an example:
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] dir /s /AD /O-D | findstr /v /i "\\.\\"
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] Explanation:
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] - `/s`: Lists all files and directories recursively.
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] - `/AD`: Lists only directories (not files).
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] - `/O-D`: Sorts by name in descending order (optional, adjust as needed).
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] - `findstr /v /i "\\.\\"`: Excludes (`/v`) lines containing `\.` (escaped dot) case-insensitively
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] (`/i`).
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] This will ignore directories like `.git`, `.vscode`, etc. Let me know if you'd like further
2025-08-09 13:00:45 [deepseek-chat] adjustments!
