2025-08-09 15:51:04 [deepseek-chat] Yes, you can use the `git ls-remote` command to check if a GitHub repository is private. Here's
2025-08-09 15:51:04 [deepseek-chat] how:
2025-08-09 15:51:04 [deepseek-chat] 1. Public Repo: If the repo is public, the command will return the remote references
2025-08-09 15:51:04 [deepseek-chat] (branches, tags, etc.).
2025-08-09 15:51:04 [deepseek-chat]    git ls-remote https://github.com/username/repo.git
2025-08-09 15:51:04 [deepseek-chat] 2. Private Repo: If the repo is private and you don't have access, the command will prompt
2025-08-09 15:51:04 [deepseek-chat] for credentials or fail with an authentication error.
2025-08-09 15:51:04 [deepseek-chat] Alternatively, you can use the GitHub API or tools like `gh` (GitHub CLI) to check repository
2025-08-09 15:51:04 [deepseek-chat] visibility. Let me know if you'd like details on those methods.
