2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] some of the elements in your arrays contain the Unicode replacement character �, which means those
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] entries are invalid – either the code unit sequence was corrupted or the characters failed
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] to encode correctly.
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] The correct mathematical-sans bold alphabet has these hex values:
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] upper
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] `U+1D5D4..U+1D5ED` (e.g. "𝗔" = U+1D5D4, "𝗕" = U+1D5D5 … "𝗭" = U+1D5ED)
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] lower
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] `U+1D5EE..U+1D607` ("𝗮" = U+1D5EE, "𝗯" = U+1D5EF … "𝘇" = U+1D607)
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] digits
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] `U+1D7CE..U+1D7D7` ("𝟬" = U+1D7CE … "𝟵" = U+1D7D7)
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] Replace the garbled entries with their correct characters (or just re-copy from the above) and your
2025-08-10 13:18:56 [kimi-k2-turbo] function will work reliably everywhere—no replacement characters, no platform-dependent surprises.
2025-08-10 13:27:02 [nitro@ryzen5] js string to array of chars
2025-08-10 13:27:04 [kimi-k2-turbo] In JavaScript, every string is already iterable, so the shortest way to get an array of the
2025-08-10 13:27:04 [kimi-k2-turbo] individual characters is:
2025-08-10 13:27:04 [kimi-k2-turbo] const chars = [...str];   // or Array.from(str)
2025-08-10 13:27:04 [kimi-k2-turbo] Both spread (`[...]`) and `Array.from` honor Unicode code-points, so even Emojis or astral-plane
2025-08-10 13:27:04 [kimi-k2-turbo] characters come back as complete glyphs.
