2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] Sounds like the issue is the enumeration order (uppercase keys coming before lowercase). Two short
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] fixes depending on what you want:
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] 1) Preserve the JSON insertion order (no sorting — recommended if you want the same order that was
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] in the JSON):
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] for (const [key, codes] of Object.entries(item.alphabet)) {
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] 	echo(tag, key, codes);
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] }
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] 2) Force case‑insensitive ordering (treat "A" and "a" the same, and order alphabetically ignoring
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] case):
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] Object.keys(item.alphabet)
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] 	.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, undefined, { sensitivity: 'base' }))
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] 	.forEach(key => {
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] 		const codes = item.alphabet[key];
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] 		echo(tag, key, codes);
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] 	});
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] Notes:
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] - Prefer Object.keys/Object.entries over for...in (for...in can enumerate inherited properties and
2025-08-10 15:53:48 [gpt-5-mini] is less explicit).
