2025-08-10 16:07:01 [gpt-5-mini] Clarification:
2025-08-10 16:07:01 [gpt-5-mini] - In a broad family sense it’s an “alphabetic” system (letters represent phonemes), but in the
2025-08-10 16:07:01 [gpt-5-mini] strict sense it does not mark vowels regularly, so it’s not a full (vowel‑marking) alphabet like
2025-08-10 16:07:01 [gpt-5-mini] Greek or Latin.
2025-08-10 16:07:01 [gpt-5-mini] - Precise tags to use: "abjad", "consonantal", "unicameral", "RTL". You can keep "alphabetic" if
2025-08-10 16:07:01 [gpt-5-mini] you want a broader category, but "abjad" is more accurate.
2025-08-10 16:08:49 [nitro@ryzen5] invented by?
2025-08-10 16:08:54 [gpt-5-mini] Not a single inventor. Phoenician evolved from earlier Semitic scripts:
2025-08-10 16:08:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Origins: developed from Proto‑Sinaitic / Proto‑Canaanite inscriptions, which themselves adapted
2025-08-10 16:08:54 [gpt-5-mini] elements of Egyptian hieroglyphs.
2025-08-10 16:08:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Agents: Canaanite/Phoenician scribes and traders standardized the script.
2025-08-10 16:08:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Date: roughly late 2nd — early 1st millennium BCE (commonly c. 12th–8th centuries BCE), with
2025-08-10 16:08:54 [gpt-5-mini] Phoenician forms well attested by the 11th–8th c. BCE.
2025-08-10 16:08:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Note: exact development details remain debated among scholars.
