2025-08-11 11:09:06 [nitro@ryzen5] wow debouncing, that is cool approach, is that common?
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] Yes — debouncing is a common pragmatic workaround, but not the most robust.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] Quick summary:
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Debouncing: wait a short time after an Enter (or keystroke) to let the rest of a pasted chunk
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] arrive, then process the whole buffer at once. It’s simple and often effective across terminals
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] that split pasted data into multiple read() calls.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Where it’s used: GUIs, network input, key-repeat handling and some terminal apps use it when a
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] higher-level paste signal isn’t available.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] Pros of the debounce approach:
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Easy to implement.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Works on many terminals without special terminal features.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Low code churn.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] Cons / risks:
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Requires tuning (too short → still split; too long → noticeable delay for normal Enter).
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Can delay legitimate single-line Enter presses.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Not 100% reliable across all terminals/OSes and remote sessions.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] A more robust alternative (recommended):
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Enable and handle "bracketed paste" (supported by xterm/most modern terminals). The terminal
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] wraps pasted text with ESC[200~ … ESC[201~ so you can detect paste boundaries precisely and avoid
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] any timing heuristics.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Fall back to debounce if bracketed paste isn’t available.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] Practical advice:
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - If you stick with debounce, 20–100 ms is common; 40 ms (what you used) is a reasonable default.
2025-08-11 11:09:13 [gpt-5-mini] - Prefer implementing bracketed-paste handling where possible and keep debounce as a fallback.
