2025-08-11 11:10:00 [nitro@ryzen5] how do i enable bracketed paste?
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] Short answer: send the terminal the "enable bracketed paste" control sequence and watch for the
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] paste markers when reading stdin.
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] Details:
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] - Enable: write ESC[?2004h (bytes: 0x1B, '[', '?', '2', '0', '0', '4', 'h')
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] - Disable: write ESC[?2004l
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] - When enabled, pasted text is wrapped by:
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini]   - start marker ESC[200~  (bytes 0x1B,'[','2','0','0','~')
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini]   - end marker   ESC[201~  (bytes 0x1B,'[','2','0','1','~')
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] How to use in your app:
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] - Send the enable sequence to the terminal (e.g., writer.write(encoder.encode("\x1B[?2004h")) )
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] when you enter raw mode (or on program start).
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] - In your raw stdin read loop, detect the start marker; when seen, read bytes until you see the end
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] marker, treat everything between as a single pasted chunk (do not split on newlines inside it).
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] - When finished or on exit, send the disable sequence.
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] Notes and tips:
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] - Always keep a debounce fallback for terminals that don’t support bracketed paste (older or remote
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] terminals).
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] - Enable bracketed paste only when you intend to read raw input and disable it before handing
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] control back to other programs.
2025-08-11 11:10:09 [gpt-5-mini] - Some terminal multiplexers / remote shells may interfere; test locally first.
