2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] Got it — avoid "apply" side-effect methods. Here’s a small, safer design that keeps Personas as
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] plain data and uses pure lookups/merges instead of mutating runtime state.
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] Core idea (summary)
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] - Make Persona a plain object (no applyToRuntime/applyToPayload).
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] - Provide a single pure selector function getActiveConfig() that returns the effective runtime
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] config by merging:
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini]   defaults <- activePersona <- per-request overrides
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] - relay() and other callers read config from getActiveConfig() each call and build their payloads
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] from that result.
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] - Update CLI handlers to modify the active persona object and persist roha.personas; do not mutate
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] grok globals directly. Keep grok as deprecated mirrors if you want, but derive them from
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] getActiveConfig() when needed.
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] Why this is better
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] - No hidden side effects; configuration is computed, not applied.
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] - Easier to reason about and test: getActiveConfig(personas, overrides) -> config
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] - Per-request behavior is explicit: callers pass overrides rather than rely on global mutation.
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] - Backward-compatibility: you can implement getActiveConfig() to fall back to legacy grok* values
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] when roha.personas is absent.
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] Suggested data shapes
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini] - Persona {
2025-08-11 11:30:47 [gpt-5-mini]     id: string,
