2025-08-12 09:04:45 [nitro@ryzen5] should clonerepos refresh or should i put that in separated bat
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] I’d recommend an idempotent single script (e.g., clonerepos) that does both: clone missing repos
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] and refresh (pull) existing ones. That’s usually the most convenient and safe approach.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] Why:
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - One command to run for first-time setup and ongoing sync.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Avoids duplicate logic and juggling separate scripts.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Easier to schedule (cron/Task Scheduler) or run manually.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] Suggested behavior (high level):
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Keep repo URLs (or name+URL) in a list/file.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - For each entry:
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini]   - If folder missing → git clone (optionally --depth 1 for speed).
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini]   - If folder exists → git -C <dir> fetch && git -C <dir> pull --ff-only (or git pull --rebase) to
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] avoid unwanted merge commits.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Handle errors/logging, optionally run a dry-run mode.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Consider submodules (git submodule update --init --recursive) if you use them.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - For many repos, consider parallel updates (PowerShell jobs, GNU parallel) to speed it up.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - Use SSH keys or token-based auth; avoid embedding credentials in scripts.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] Notes:
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - If you prefer clear separation: make a small clone-only script and an update-only script, but a
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] combined, idempotent script is usually best.
2025-08-12 09:04:54 [gpt-5-mini] - On Windows, PowerShell is more powerful and safer than a plain .bat file.
2025-08-12 09:04:55 [gpt-5-mini] Want a sample script? Tell me platform (Windows PowerShell, .bat, or bash) and I’ll produce one.
