2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] Yes — you can suppress nearly all output by redirecting git and pushd/popd output to nul and only
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] printing on error. Paste this into nito_refresh.bat (or refresh.bat):
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] @echo off
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] setlocal
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] for /D %%D in ("nitrologic\*") do (
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] 	if exist "%%D\.git" (
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] 		pushd "%%D" >nul
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] 		git pull --quiet >nul 2>&1
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] 		if errorlevel 1 echo Failed: %%~nxD
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] 		popd >nul
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] 	)
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] )
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] endlocal
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] Notes
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] - git pull --quiet suppresses normal git output; redirecting >nul 2>&1 hides any remaining
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] stdout/stderr.
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] - The script only echoes when a git pull fails. Remove the if errorlevel block if you want complete
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] silence.
2025-08-12 09:20:15 [gpt-5-mini] - Optionally replace git pull with git pull --ff-only if you want to avoid merges and fail instead.
