2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] Yes — use git's -C (or --git-dir / --work-tree) so you never change cwd.
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] Windows batch (use inside a .bat file; note doubled %% for batch loops):
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] for /d %%D in ("nitrologic\*") do (
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	echo Updating %%~nxD
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	if exist "%%D\.git" (
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 		"C:\Program Files\Git\bin\git.exe" -C "%%D" pull --ff-only || echo Failed to pull %%~nxD
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	) else (
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 		echo Skipping %%~nxD (no .git)
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	)
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] )
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] PowerShell:
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] Get-ChildItem -Directory nitrologic | ForEach-Object {
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	if (Test-Path (Join-Path $_.FullName ".git")) {
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 		git -C $.FullName pull --ff-only || Write-Host "Failed: $($.Name)"
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	} else {
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 		Write-Host "Skipping $($_.Name) (no .git)"
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	}
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] }
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] Bash:
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] for d in nitrologic/*/; do
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	[ -d "$d/.git" ] || continue
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	echo "Updating ${d%/}"
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] 	git -C "$d" pull --ff-only || echo "Failed: $d"
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] done
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] Use --ff-only if you want to avoid unintended merges; remove it if you want normal merge behavior.
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] If you need to handle repos whose default branch is main vs master, add a git -C "$d" fetch origin
2025-08-12 09:25:57 [gpt-5-mini] and git -C "$d" remote show origin to inspect and fix tracking.
2025-08-12 09:27:32 [nitro@ryzen5] what is setlocal?
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] setlocal is a cmd.exe batch command that creates a local scope for environment changes. When you
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] run setlocal, any subsequent SET, PATH, or other environment modifications are confined to that
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] scope and are reverted when an explicit endlocal runs or when the batch file exits.
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] Key points:
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] - Purpose: avoid permanently changing the caller's environment (and avoid leaking variables between
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] scripts).
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] - Scope: lasts until endlocal is executed or the batch script ends.
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] - Options: you can enable features when starting setlocal, e.g. ENABLEDELAYEDEXPANSION (for !var!
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] syntax) and ENABLEEXTENSIONS.
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] - Typical use: wrap a chunk of a script where you need temporary variables or change enabled
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] features.
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] - Caveats: percent (%) expansion happens before runtime; when using delayed expansion or carrying a
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] value out of a setlocal block you need to account for expansion timing.
2025-08-12 09:27:41 [gpt-5-mini] If you want, I can show a short example of using setlocal/endlocal and ENABLEDELAYEDEXPANSION.
