2025-08-12 09:32:47 [nitro@ryzen5] is it easy to make the nitrologic folder readonly?
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] Yes — simple to do, but pick the right method for your goal.
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] Options
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] - Folder Properties (GUI): Right‑click the folder → Properties → Security → Edit. Remove the
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] Write/Modify permissions for the users/groups you want to prevent from changing files. Quick and
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] safe for per‑user control.
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] - NTFS ACL (recommended CLI): Use icacls to remove write access or explicitly deny it for specific
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] accounts or groups. This enforces read‑only at the filesystem level.
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] - Mount/read‑only media: Put the folder on a read‑only volume (ISO/VHD mounted read‑only or
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] physically write‑protected drive) — good if you want to prevent all changes, including by services.
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] - attrib +r: Not reliable for protecting folder contents on modern Windows (Explorer treats the
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] folder ReadOnly bit differently), so don’t rely on it.
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] Warnings
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] - Administrators (or SYSTEM) can always restore permissions. You cannot make it absolutely
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] immutable for admins without changing ownership or using additional controls.
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] - Making the folder read‑only will break any tools that need to write (git pull, builds, editors).
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] If you still want to update occasionally, consider setting read‑only and temporarily restoring
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] write permissions when needed.
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] Would you like the exact icacls or PowerShell commands to make C:\nitrologic read‑only for standard
2025-08-12 09:32:55 [gpt-5-mini] users (while keeping Administrators intact)? If so, tell me which accounts/groups to restrict
