2025-08-12 09:33:34 [nitro@ryzen5] it is gitignored, i would prefer to switch on after scripts like nitro_refresh
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] Yes — easy. Two practical approaches depending on how strict you need it:
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] 1) Fast & simple (no admin): toggle the Read‑Only file attribute
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - Pros: works without elevated permissions, simple to toggle before/after your script.
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - Cons: not a security barrier (can be undone easily) and some tools ignore the attribute.
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] Example batch commands:
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] attrib +R "C:\nitrologic\*" /S /D    :: make read‑only
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] attrib -R "C:\nitrologic\*" /S /D    :: make writable
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] You can wrap them around your refresh:
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - make writable
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - run nitro_refresh
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - make read‑only
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] 2) Stronger (NTFS ACLs, admin required): adjust permissions with icacls
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - Pros: enforces write-block for standard Users.
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - Cons: requires admin to change/restore; Administrators/SYSTEM can always restore.
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] Recommended pattern: save the current ACL once, then restore it after running updates.
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] Save original ACL (run once as admin):
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] icacls "C:\nitrologic" /save "%~dp0\nitrologic_acls.acl" /t
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] Make read‑only for Users (deny write; run as admin):
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] icacls "C:\nitrologic" /deny "Users:(OI)(CI)W" /T
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] Restore original ACL from the saved file (run as admin):
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] icacls "C:\nitrologic" /restore "%~dp0\nitrologic_acls.acl"
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] Notes and recommendations
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - If you just want a convenient toggle for your refresh runs, the attrib approach is simplest.
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - If you need actual protection from non-admin accounts, use the icacls approach and run the
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] wrapper script as admin.
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] - Always test on a small sample folder before applying to the whole tree.
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] Which would you like: a ready-to-run wrapper batch that toggles attributes around your
2025-08-12 09:33:50 [gpt-5-mini] nitro_refresh, or the ACL-based wrapper (with ACL save/restore)?
2025-08-12 09:34:10 [nitro@ryzen5] what is ::
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] In Windows batch, :: is a common shorthand for a comment line. It’s treated like a label (a line
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] beginning with :) so the command processor ignores it — effectively a comment.
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] Key differences vs REM
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] - REM is the official comment command and works reliably everywhere (including inside parenthesized
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] blocks).
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] - :: is widely used because it’s short and often marginally faster, but it’s actually a label, not
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] a true comment. That can make it unsafe in some contexts (notably inside () blocks or in places
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] where labels can affect parsing).
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] Recommendation
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] - Use :: for simple, top-level comments.
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] - Use REM when you need maximum compatibility and safety (especially inside parentheses or complex
2025-08-12 09:34:26 [gpt-5-mini] scripts).
