2025-08-12 19:18:50 [nitro@ryzen5] in dos simplest way to see how big in bytes a folder is
2025-08-12 19:19:02 [grok-4-0709] In Windows Command Prompt (DOS-like), use:
2025-08-12 19:19:02 [grok-4-0709] `dir /s foldername`
2025-08-12 19:19:02 [grok-4-0709] This lists contents recursively and shows total file bytes at the end. For just the size (no
2025-08-12 19:19:02 [grok-4-0709] listing), consider PowerShell: `(Get-ChildItem foldername -Recurse | Measure-Object -Property
2025-08-12 19:19:02 [grok-4-0709] Length -Sum).Sum`
2025-08-12 19:19:54 [nitro@ryzen5] what does /-c /A-D-H do?
2025-08-12 19:20:08 [grok-4-0709] In the Windows `dir` command:
2025-08-12 19:20:08 [grok-4-0709] - `/-c`: Disables the display of thousand separators (commas) in file sizes (opposite of `/c`,
2025-08-12 19:20:08 [grok-4-0709] which enables them).
2025-08-12 19:20:08 [grok-4-0709] - `/A-D-H`: Filters the listing to show only files that are not directories (`-D`) and
2025-08-12 19:20:08 [grok-4-0709] not hidden (`-H`).
2025-08-12 19:20:08 [grok-4-0709] These are often combined (e.g., `dir /s /-c /A-D-H foldername`) to recursively list non-hidden
2025-08-12 19:20:08 [grok-4-0709] files and sum their sizes in bytes without commas, with totals at the end. For exact folder byte
2025-08-12 19:20:08 [grok-4-0709] size, parse the "bytes" line or use tools like PowerShell for precision.
