2025-08-13 08:15:19 [nitro@ryzen5] what did sinclair computers use?
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] [skid] Sinclair computers had minimalist sound hardware, evolving across models:
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] - ZX81 (1981): No dedicated audio IC—sound was absent or hacked via software bit-banging to
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] pulse the TV speaker (e.g., crude beeps from Z80 CPU I/O).
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] - ZX Spectrum (1982): Basic single-channel beeper via the ULA (Ferranti custom chip) for
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] square-wave tones. No true sound chip; relied on CPU-driven output.
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] - ZX Spectrum 128K/+2/+3 (1985-1987): Added AY-3-8910 (General Instrument) for 3-channel
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] PSG sound, with envelopes and noise—same as Amstrad CPC and MSX.
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] This fits "audio-ic" as budget evolutions. Suggest adding:
