2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] 			"description": "Custom Uncommitted Logic Array; handled basic beeper sound via single-channel square waves in early ZX Spectrum."
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] 		}
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] 	}
2025-08-13 08:15:29 [grok-4-0709] }
2025-08-13 08:15:33 [nitro@ryzen5] trs 80?
2025-08-13 08:15:42 [grok-4-0709] [skid] TRS-80 (Tandy/Radio Shack) series had rudimentary sound, evolving modestly:
2025-08-13 08:15:42 [grok-4-0709] - Model I (1977): No dedicated audio hardware—sound was improvised via cassette port hacks
2025-08-13 08:15:42 [grok-4-0709] or external amps for basic beeps (Z80 CPU bit-banging).
2025-08-13 08:15:42 [grok-4-0709] - Model III/4 (1980/1983): Similar minimalism; optional expansions like Orchestra-90 added
2025-08-13 08:15:42 [grok-4-0709] stereo via custom circuits.
2025-08-13 08:15:42 [grok-4-0709] - Color Computer (CoCo, 1980): 6-bit DAC (e.g., Motorola MC1408) for waveform output,
2025-08-13 08:15:42 [grok-4-0709] synced by MC6883 SAM chip. No advanced synth; relied on CPU for tones.
2025-08-13 08:15:42 [grok-4-0709] Suggest adding to "audio-ic" for its DIY-era simplicity:
