2025-08-13 12:53:07 [nitro@ryzen5] in deno using normal prompt can i catch any keyboard activity, say ctrl c?
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] In Deno, you can listen for keyboard events using the `Deno.stdin.setRaw` mode. Here's a basic
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] example to catch Ctrl+C (SIGINT):
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] Deno.stdin.setRaw(true);
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] const buf = new Uint8Array(1);
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] while (true) {
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat]   const n = await Deno.stdin.read(buf);
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat]   if (n === null) break; // EOF
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat]   const char = buf[0];
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat]   if (char === 3) { // Ctrl+C
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat]     console.log("Ctrl+C detected");
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat]     break;
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat]   }
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] Note:
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] 1. Requires `--unstable` flag
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] 2. `setRaw` mode gives you raw keypress events
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] 3. Ctrl+C is ASCII code 3
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] 4. Don't forget to handle cleanup with `Deno.stdin.setRaw(false)` when done
2025-08-13 12:53:17 [deepseek-chat] Would you like me to expand on any part of this?
2025-08-13 12:53:45 [nitro@ryzen5] yeh nah, how about cursor up down?
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] In Deno's raw mode, you can detect cursor keys by checking for ANSI escape sequences. Here's how to
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] detect up/down arrows:
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] Deno.stdin.setRaw(true);
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] const buf = new Uint8Array(3); // Arrow keys send 3 bytes
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] while (true) {
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat]   const n = await Deno.stdin.read(buf);
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat]   if (n === null) break;
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat]   // Check for arrow key sequence: ESC [ A (up) or ESC [ B (down)
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat]   if (buf[0] === 27 && buf[1] === 91) {
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat]     if (buf[2] === 65) console.log("Up arrow");
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat]     if (buf[2] === 66) console.log("Down arrow");
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat]   }
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] Arrow keys typically send:
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] - Up: `\x1b[A`
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] - Down: `\x1b[B`
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] - Right: `\x1b[C`
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] - Left: `\x1b[D`
2025-08-13 12:53:56 [deepseek-chat] Remember to run with `--unstable` flag.
2025-08-13 12:54:28 [nitro@ryzen5] which unstable flag?
2025-08-13 12:54:35 [deepseek-chat] In Deno, you need to use the `--unstable` flag because the `Deno.stdin.setRaw()` method is
2025-08-13 12:54:35 [deepseek-chat] considered unstable API. Here's how to run your script:
2025-08-13 12:54:35 [deepseek-chat] deno run --unstable your_script.ts
2025-08-13 12:54:35 [deepseek-chat] The flag enables access to:
2025-08-13 12:54:35 [deepseek-chat] 1. Raw mode terminal input (`setRaw`)
2025-08-13 12:54:35 [deepseek-chat] 2. Other experimental Deno APIs
2025-08-13 12:54:35 [deepseek-chat] 3. Features that may change in future versions
2025-08-13 12:54:35 [deepseek-chat] Without this flag, you'll get an error like:
2025-08-13 12:54:35 [deepseek-chat] `Uncaught TypeError: Deno.stdin.setRaw is not a function`
