2025-08-13 12:58:03 [nitro@ryzen5] midi?
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] You can use MIDI in Deno via the Web MIDI API (supported in Deno's
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] browser-compatible runtime) or by interfacing with native MIDI libraries through FFI
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] (Foreign Function Interface). Here's how:
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Option 1: Web MIDI API (Browser-Compatible)
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Deno supports the [Web MIDI
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebMIDIAPI), which allows MIDI device
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] communication in a browser-like way.
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Example: List MIDI Devices
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] if (!("requestMIDIAccess" in navigator)) {
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   console.error("Web MIDI API not supported in this environment.");
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   Deno.exit(1);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] const midiAccess = await navigator.requestMIDIAccess();
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] console.log("MIDI Devices:");
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] // List input devices (e.g., MIDI keyboards)
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] midiAccess.inputs.forEach((input) => {
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   console.log(`Input: ${input.name} (${input.manufacturer})`);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   input.onmidimessage = (event) => {
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]     console.log("MIDI Message:", event.data);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   };
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] });
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] // List output devices (e.g., synthesizers)
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] midiAccess.outputs.forEach((output) => {
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   console.log(`Output: ${output.name} (${output.manufacturer})`);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] });
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Example: Send a MIDI Note
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] const midiAccess = await navigator.requestMIDIAccess();
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] const outputs = midiAccess.outputs.values();
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] for (const output of outputs) {
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   // Send a MIDI "Note On" message (channel 0, note 60, velocity 127)
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   output.send([0x90, 60, 127]);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   // Turn it off after 500ms
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   setTimeout(() => {
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]     output.send([0x80, 60, 0]);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   }, 500);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Option 2: Native MIDI via FFI (Deno FFI)
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] If you need low-level MIDI control, you can use Deno's FFI to call native MIDI
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] libraries (e.g., ALSA on Linux, CoreMIDI on macOS, WinMM on Windows).
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Example: Using `portmidi` via FFI
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] 1. Install `portmidi` (cross-platform MIDI library):
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    # macOS
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    brew install portmidi
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    # Linux (Debian/Ubuntu)
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    sudo apt-get install libportmidi-dev
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    # Windows (via vcpkg or manual build)
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] 2. Load `portmidi` in Deno:
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    const dylib = Deno.dlopen(
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]      Deno.build.os === "darwin" ? "libportmidi.dylib" :
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]      Deno.build.os === "linux" ? "libportmidi.so" :
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]      "portmidi.dll",
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]      {
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]        "Pm_Initialize": { parameters: [], result: "i32" },
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]        "Pm_CountDevices": { parameters: [], result: "i32" },
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]        "Pm_GetDeviceInfo": { parameters: ["i32"], result: "pointer" },
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]      }
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    );
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    dylib.symbols.Pm_Initialize();
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    const deviceCount = dylib.symbols.Pm_CountDevices();
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]    console.log(`Found ${deviceCount} MIDI devices.`);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Option 3: Deno + Node.js MIDI Libraries (via `npm:`)
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] If you need higher-level MIDI control, you can use Node.js MIDI libraries via
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] `npm:` specifiers.
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Example: Using `easymidi`
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] import { createRequire } from "https://deno.land/std/node/module.ts";
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] const easymidi = require("easymidi");
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] // List inputs/outputs
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] console.log("Inputs:", easymidi.getInputs());
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] console.log("Outputs:", easymidi.getOutputs());
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] // Create a virtual MIDI input
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] const input = new easymidi.Input("Deno MIDI Input");
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] input.on("noteon", (msg) => {
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat]   console.log(`Note ${msg.note} pressed with velocity ${msg.velocity}`);
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] });
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Which Should You Use?
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] ┌──────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────┐
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] │  Method                      │  Pros                          │  Cons                                     │
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] ├──────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] │  **Web MIDI API**            │  ✅ Browser-compatible, simple  │  ❌ Limited to browser-like MIDI features  │
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] │  **FFI (Native MIDI)**       │  ✅ Full control, low latency   │  ❌ Complex, platform-specific             │
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] │  **Node.js MIDI (`npm:`) **  │  ✅ Easy, high-level            │  ❌ Requires `--unstable` & npm            │
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] └──────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────┘
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] Next Steps
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] - If you just need basic MIDI I/O, use Web MIDI API.
2025-08-13 12:58:50 [deepseek-chat] - If you need low-level control, use FFI + `portmidi`.
