2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] Here’s a breakdown of these Unicode space/control characters with their proper names and common
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] uses:
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] 1. Thin Space (`\u2009`)
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Name: THIN SPACE
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Width: ~1/5 of an em space (narrower than a regular space).
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Use: Typographic spacing (e.g., between punctuation in French: `« Bonjour »`).
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] 2. Hair Space (`\u200A`)
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Name: HAIR SPACE
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Width: Thinner than thin space (~1/10–1/16 of an em).
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Use: Fine kerning (e.g., between letters in logos or tight typography).
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] 3. Zero Width Space (`\u200B`)
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Name: ZERO WIDTH SPACE
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Width: Invisible, but allows line-breaking.
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Use: Word separation without visual space (e.g., long URLs in text).
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] 4. Zero Width Non-Joiner (`\u200C`)
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Name: ZERO WIDTH NON-JOINER (ZWNJ)
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Width: Invisible, blocks ligatures/joining.
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Use: Prevents cursive connection (e.g., Arabic `فا‌ن` vs. `فان`).
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] 5. Zero Width Joiner (`\u200D`)
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Name: ZERO WIDTH JOINER (ZWJ)
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Width: Invisible, forces connection.
2025-08-13 13:16:01 [deepseek-chat] - Use: Emoji sequences (👨‍👩‍👧‍👦) or script joining (e.g., Indic/Sinhala).
