2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] Yes.
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] What Windows provides
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - SAPI (Speech API) — classic COM-based TTS and recognition (sapi.dll).
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - WinRT/UWP APIs — Windows.Media.SpeechSynthesis and Windows.Media.SpeechRecognition.
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Microsoft Speech SDK / Azure Speech — cloud SDKs (and REST/WebSocket) for TTS/STT (higher
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] accuracy, cross-platform).
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - WASAPI — audio input/output (not TTS/STT itself).
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] How to call them from Deno
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Easiest / fastest: invoke a local helper process from Deno (spawn PowerShell, a small C#/Rust
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] exe, or Node script) that uses SAPI or WinRT. (Deno run with subprocess permissions.)
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Direct native: use Deno FFI (Deno.dlopen) to call native DLLs or a C wrapper around COM/WinRT —
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] possible but more work.
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Cloud: call Azure Speech REST or WebSocket APIs from Deno (no native bindings needed).
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] Recommended choices
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Quick local TTS proof-of-concept: call PowerShell or a tiny helper program that uses
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] SAPI/Windows.Media.SpeechSynthesis.
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - Production or cross-platform: use Azure Speech SDK (REST/WS) from Deno.
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] - If you need direct low-level integration in-process, use Deno FFI + a small native wrapper to
2025-08-13 14:35:57 [gpt-5-mini] handle COM/WinRT complexity.
