2025-08-13 14:56:45 [nitro@ryzen5] nice, i have thoughts that if i was to run two instances of slopfountain on same local account I may have issues?
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] Short version: you’ll corrupt state (shares, counters, log timestamps, cached balances)
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] because both instances will overwrite the same `forge.json` and `forge.log` on disk every
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] time they:
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] • push `/commit` a file
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] • settle usage credits
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] • count relays or sessions
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] • flush console output to the log
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] For bullet-proof concurrency you either:
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] 1. separate workdirs (need a copy of the repo per instance, then set `DENO_DIR`, `TD`, or a
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] `--config folder/` arg) or
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] 2. lock the file (use Deno’s `Deno.open` with write-lock, or lightweight IPC via a
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] `lock.json` with the PID inside) or
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] 3. go stateless – let every instance open the saved JSON, import, and emit
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] `forge-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.json` instead of the single name.
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] Option 1 is the least code: just open two terminals with
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711]    `cd slopfountain-1 && deno run ...`
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711]    and
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711]    `cd slopfountain-2 && deno run ...`
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] Option 2 takes a ~25-line wrapper that `tryLockSync()`s `forge.json` before writes (easy, but one
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] instance blocks).
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] Most folk pick option 1; the caches are cheap to clone, and you avoid any merge conflicts on shared
2025-08-13 14:57:19 [kimi-k2-0711] counters.
2025-08-13 14:59:21 [nitro@ryzen5] there is an explicit lock for the entire app whenever a prompt takes 25 seconds to reply yes?
2025-08-13 14:59:31 [kimi-k2-0711] No, there’s no builtin lock around the full app today.
2025-08-13 14:59:31 [kimi-k2-0711] A single long-running prompt only blocks its own thread; another process can still
2025-08-13 14:59:31 [kimi-k2-0711] read/write the same `forge.json` while the first is waiting. The 25 s artificial delay
2025-08-13 14:59:31 [kimi-k2-0711] (`roha.config.slow`) is just inserted in the console output loop – it doesn’t raise any client-side
2025-08-13 14:59:31 [kimi-k2-0711] advisory lock.
2025-08-13 14:59:31 [kimi-k2-0711] So concurrency races (lost edits, duplicated counters, mangled log) will still happen unless you
2025-08-13 14:59:31 [kimi-k2-0711] add a real lock or separate paths.
2025-08-13 14:59:55 [nitro@ryzen5] i noticed i had to quit with ctrl c and cannot interupt api calls from slop fountain
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711] Exactly—right now the relay loop waits synchronously for the fetch, so pressing Ctrl-C just kills
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711] the whole process instead of interrupting the in-flight request.
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711] You can make it cancelable by adding an `AbortController`:
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711] 1. In `relay()`:
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]    const aborter = new AbortController();
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]    const timer = setTimeout(() => aborter.abort(), 30_000);   // optional timeout
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]    const completion = await endpoint.chat.completions.create({
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]        ...payload,
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]        signal: aborter.signal
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]    });
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]    clearTimeout(timer);
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711] 2. Wire the signal to a listener that triggers on keypress.
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711] Quick hack:
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]    const rl = readline.createInterface({input: Deno.stdin});
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]    rl.on('keypress', (_char, key) => {
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]        if (key.ctrl && key.name == 'c') aborter.abort();
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711]    });
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711] With that, Ctrl-C while a model is thinking will `AbortSignal.throwIfAborted()` cleanly instead of
2025-08-13 15:00:14 [kimi-k2-0711] killing Deno.
2025-08-13 15:00:54 [nitro@ryzen5] but if slopfountain is to be a many to many, how can I allow multiple models to think at same time?
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] You already have the infrastructure — the challenge is just scheduling.
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] The “many-to-many” idea means:
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] 1. independent chat sessions (each with its own history, model, token budget) can live
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] side-by-side, and
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] 2. one session can still interleave multiple asynchronous model calls (tool-use,
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] reflection, sub-queries, etc.).
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] Design sketch that stays close to your current code-base:
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] A.  Turn every user interaction into a session record
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] class Session {
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     id: string = crypto.randomUUID();
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     rohaHistory: Plop[] = [];
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     modelName: string;
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     abort = new AbortController();
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] }
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] const sessions = new Map<string, Session>();   // key = sessionId
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] B.  A tiny task queue for relay-tasks
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] import { pooledMap } from "https://deno.land/std/async/pool.ts";
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] async function runTask(task: () => Promise<void>) {
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     await pooledMap(4, [task], (t) => t());  // 4 concurrent relays max
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] }
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] C.  Replace the single global `relay(depth)` with per-session, cancellable relayer
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] async function relaySession(session: Session) {
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     if (session.abort.signal.aborted) return;
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     try {
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]         const payload = buildPayload(session);   // use the session’s history
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]         const completion = await endpoint.chat.completions.create({
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]             ...payload,
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]             signal: session.abort.signal
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]         });
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]         appendToSessionHistory(session, completion);
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     } catch (e) {
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]         if (e.name === "AbortError") echo("session", session.id, "cancelled");
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]         else throw e;
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     }
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] }
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] D.  A dispatcher loop listening on a local pipe / port
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] const listener = Deno.listen({ port: 8081 });
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] for await (const conn of listener) {
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     const session = new Session(await greeting(conn));
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     sessions.set(session.id, session);
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     runTask(() => chatLoop(session, conn));   // each chat gets its own async loop
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] }
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] E.  Interruption strategy
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] // drain stdin for only global commands:  exit, abort session
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] Deno.addSignalListener("SIGINT", () => {
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     const id = prompt("Enter session to abort (or 'exit')");
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711]     sessions.get(id)?.abort.abort();
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] });
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] Result
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] • Any incoming socket connection (or new command-line invocation) fabricates a new `Session`;
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] thousands can coexist.
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] • Each model call runs concurrently via the pooled task queue.
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] • Ctrl-C only cancels the session you ask for; others continue thinking.
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] • Shared `forge.json` writes are still file-based, so either keep separate directories or
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] wrap `writeForge()` with a simple file-lock (one line with `Deno.open` and `exclusive: true`).
2025-08-13 15:01:41 [kimi-k2-0711] All changes are additive to your current file; you’re already ~90 % there.
