2025-08-14 09:48:13 [nitro@ryzen5] a dir command option in dos that orders by date
2025-08-14 09:48:33 [nitro@ryzen5] sync is on
2025-08-14 09:48:33 [kimi-k2-0711] Use the `/o:d` switch:
2025-08-14 09:48:33 [kimi-k2-0711] dir /o:d
2025-08-14 09:48:33 [kimi-k2-0711] Lists files ordered oldest → newest (by last-write date).
2025-08-14 09:48:33 [kimi-k2-0711] Add `-d` to reverse the order:
2025-08-14 09:48:33 [kimi-k2-0711] dir /o:-d
2025-08-14 09:48:47 [kimi-k2-0711] There's no single-switch date sort built into DOS's `dir`, but you can do it in two easy ways:
2025-08-14 09:48:47 [kimi-k2-0711] 1. Use `DIR`’s built-in `/O` flag with the date field
2025-08-14 09:48:47 [kimi-k2-0711]    For newest-first:
2025-08-14 09:48:47 [kimi-k2-0711]    dir /O-D
2025-08-14 09:48:47 [kimi-k2-0711]    For oldest-first:
2025-08-14 09:48:47 [kimi-k2-0711]    dir /OD
2025-08-14 09:48:48 [kimi-k2-0711]    (`-` reverses the order; `D` chooses the date/time field.)
2025-08-14 09:48:48 [kimi-k2-0711] 2. Verbose form
2025-08-14 09:48:48 [kimi-k2-0711]    You can also spell it out:
2025-08-14 09:48:48 [kimi-k2-0711]    dir /O:D
2025-08-14 09:48:48 [kimi-k2-0711] That works from DOS 5 onward, so even retro boxes will cooperate.
