2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] Yes, the `replaceShortCodes()` function I provided does handle multiple shortcodes on one
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] line because:
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] 1. It uses a global regex (`/g` flag) to find all `:shortcode:` patterns in the
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] input string.
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] 2. The callback runs for each match, replacing them one by one.
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] Example:
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] const text = "Hello :smile: and :heart: world!";
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] const replaced = replaceShortCodes(text);
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] // Output: "Hello 😄 and ❤️ world!"
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] How It Works:
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] - Regex: `/:([a-z_]+):/g`
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat]   - Matches `:smile:`, `:heart:`, etc.
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat]   - `([a-z_]+)` captures the inner text (e.g., `smile`).
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] - Replacement: Looks up the code in `bibli.spec.shortcode`.
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat]   - If found → replaces with the emoji (e.g., `:smile:` → `😄`).
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat]   - If not found → leaves the original text (e.g., `:unknown:` stays as-is).
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] Edge Cases Handled:
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] ✅ Multiple shortcodes in one line (`:smile: :heart:`)
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] ✅ Mixed with text (`Text :smile: more text`)
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] ✅ Unknown shortcodes (`:fake:` → remains `:fake:`)
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] Performance Note:
2025-08-14 13:39:47 [deepseek-chat] - Since it uses a single regex pass, it’s efficient even with many shortcodes.
