2025-08-16 11:20:55 [nitro@ryzen5] what is 0x5b in terminal code?
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] In terminal control codes, `0x5B` is the ASCII/Unicode value for the `[` (left square bracket)
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] character.
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] When seen in terminal escape sequences, `0x5B` typically marks the start of a Control Sequence
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] Introducer (CSI). CSI sequences are used for cursor movement, text formatting, and other
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] terminal control functions.
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] Common examples:
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] - `\x1B[` = `ESC` + `[` (start of most ANSI escape sequences)
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] - `\x1B[31m` = Red text
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] - `\x1B[2J` = Clear screen
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] - `\x1B[1;3H` = Move cursor to row 1, column 3
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] In the `rawPrompt` code you shared, `0x5B` appears when detecting arrow keys - arrow key sequences
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] typically come as `ESC [ A` (up), `ESC [ B` (down), etc.
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] So when the code sees `0x1B` (ESC) followed by `0x5B` (`[`), it knows an ANSI control sequence is
2025-08-16 11:21:10 [deepseek-chat] starting, and the next byte will indicate which specific control function to execute.
