2025-08-16 17:14:34 [nitro@ryzen5] class is only typescript?
2025-08-16 17:14:41 [deepseek-chat] No, the `class` keyword is used in both TypeScript and JavaScript.
2025-08-16 17:14:41 [deepseek-chat] The key differences are:
2025-08-16 17:14:41 [deepseek-chat] - TypeScript classes can include type annotations
2025-08-16 17:14:41 [deepseek-chat] - TypeScript supports access modifiers (public/private/protected)
2025-08-16 17:14:41 [deepseek-chat] - TypeScript classes can implement interfaces
2025-08-16 17:14:41 [deepseek-chat] But the basic class syntax works in both languages. TypeScript classes get compiled to JavaScript
2025-08-16 17:14:41 [deepseek-chat] classes.
2025-08-16 17:14:55 [nitro@ryzen5] const class?
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] In JavaScript/TypeScript, you can't use `const` with `class` directly. The correct ways to declare
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] classes are:
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] // Standard class declaration
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] class MyClass {}
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] // Class expression assigned to const
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] const MyClass = class {};
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] The `const` version is a class expression assigned to a constant variable. Both forms are valid,
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] but you can't write `const class MyClass`.
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] Key points:
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] - Class declarations are hoisted
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] - Class expressions (const version) aren't hoisted
2025-08-16 17:15:05 [deepseek-chat] - Both create classes, just with different scoping behavior
2025-08-16 17:15:20 [nitro@ryzen5] to namespace ansi consts?
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] To namespace ANSI constants (like color codes or terminal controls), you have a few good options in
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] TypeScript/JavaScript:
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] 1. Using a Plain Object:
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] const ANSI = {
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   RESET: '\x1b[0m',
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   RED: '\x1b[31m',
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   GREEN: '\x1b[32m',
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   // ... other codes
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] } as const;
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] 2. Using a Class with Static Properties:
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] class ANSI {
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   static readonly RESET = '\x1b[0m';
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   static readonly RED = '\x1b[31m';
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   // ...
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] 3. Using an Enum (TypeScript only):
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] enum ANSI {
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   RESET = '\x1b[0m',
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   RED = '\x1b[31m',
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat]   // ...
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] The `as const` in the first example makes the object properties deeply readonly. Choose based on
2025-08-16 17:15:32 [deepseek-chat] your needs - the plain object is simplest for most cases.
2025-08-16 17:15:54 [nitro@ryzen5] enum is typescript???
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat] Yes! `enum` is a TypeScript-specific feature that doesn't exist in plain JavaScript.
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat] Key points about TypeScript enums:
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat] 1. They don't exist in JavaScript (compiles to an object)
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat] 2. Three types:
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat]    enum Numeric { A, B }         // Auto-incremented numbers
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat]    enum String { A = 'a' }       // String values
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat]    enum Heterogeneous { A = 1, B = 'b' } // Mixed
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat] 3. Compiles to:
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat]    var Numeric;
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat]    (function(Numeric) {
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat]        Numeric[Numeric["A"] = 0] = "A";
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat]        Numeric[Numeric["B"] = 1] = "B";
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat]    })(Numeric || (Numeric = {}));
2025-08-16 17:16:05 [deepseek-chat] For ANSI codes, a `const enum` would be most efficient since it inlines the values.
