2025-08-16 17:18:27 [nitro@ryzen5] is the 2 suffix used anywhere in ansi terminal land?
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] No, the `2` suffix isn't part of standard ANSI escape sequences. The correct clear line sequence is
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] always `\x1B[2K` (no suffix). Here's why:
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] Standard ANSI Clear Line Codes:
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] const ANSI = {
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat]   CLEAR_LINE: '\x1B[K',    // Clears from cursor to end of line
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat]   CLEAR_LINE_FULL: '\x1B[2K', // Clears entire line
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat]   CLEAR_LINE_START: '\x1B[1K' // Clears from start to cursor
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] } as const;
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] Key ANSI Line Clearing Variants:
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] 1. `\x1B[K` - Default clear (cursor to end)
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] 2. `\x1B[1K` - Clear start to cursor
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] 3. `\x1B[2K` - Clear entire line (most common)
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] The number is part of the escape sequence itself, not a suffix. Your original `CLEAR_LINE2`
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] suggests a non-standard variation - terminal emulators wouldn't recognize it. Stick to the standard
2025-08-16 17:18:41 [deepseek-chat] `[K`, `[1K`, or `[2K` forms.
