2025-08-19 13:09:18 [nitro@ryzen5] is there ansi to set tabwidth?
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] Yes! You can use ANSI escape sequences to set the tab width in terminal emulators that support it.
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] The relevant sequence is:
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] ESC [ <n> g
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] Where `<n>` is:
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] - `0` for default tab width (usually 8)
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] - `3` to set tab stops every 3 columns
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] - Any other number to specify your desired width
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] Example in Bash:
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] printf '\e[4g'  # Sets tab width to 4 columns
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] Note: Support varies by terminal emulator. Most modern terminals (like iTerm2, Kitty, etc.) support
2025-08-19 13:09:27 [deepseek-chat] this, but basic terminals may not.
