2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] You're absolutely right. Parsing ANSI escape sequences, especially in a stream like terminal input,
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] doesn't have a single simple rule because the sequences are variable-length and context-dependent.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] The terminator is indeed a key part of the grammar.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] Here’s a robust strategy for detecting and parsing these sequences in a stream:
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 1. Understand the General Form
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] Most sequences you'll encounter follow one of these patterns:
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • `ESC [ Pn ... Pn F` where `F` is a final byte (typically a letter or `~`).
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • Some are even simpler: `ESC [ F` (like `ESC [ A` for up arrow).
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • The ones you posted are `ESC [ Pn ~` where `Pn` is a parameter number (like `18`, `19`).
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 2. Implement a State Machine
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] The most reliable way to parse a stream of bytes is with a state machine. You process one byte at a
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] time and change state based on what you receive.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] Here's a simplified example in JavaScript-like pseudocode:
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] // States for the parser
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] const STATE_IDLE = 0;       // Waiting for an ESC
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] const STATE_ESC = 1;        // Received ESC, expecting '['
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] const STATE_CSI = 2;        // Received '[', now reading parameters and waiting for final byte
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] const STATE_OSC = 3;        // OSC sequences (ESC ] ... ST)
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] // ... other states for other sequence types
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] let currentState = STATE_IDLE;
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] let currentSequence = []; // Buffer for the current sequence bytes
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] let params = [];         // Array to store parsed parameters
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] function processByte(byte) {
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]   switch (currentState) {
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]     case STATE_IDLE:
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       if (byte === 0x1B) { // ESC
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         currentState = STATE_ESC;
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         currentSequence = [byte];
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       } else {
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         // This is a regular, printable character
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         handlePlainText(byte);
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       }
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       break;
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]     case STATE_ESC:
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       currentSequence.push(byte);
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       if (byte === 0x5B) { // '['
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         currentState = STATE_CSI;
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         params = []; // Reset parameters for new CSI sequence
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       } else if (byte === 0x5D) { // ']' (Start of OSC sequence)
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         currentState = STATE_OSC;
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       }
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       // Handle other possibilities (ESC O, ESC _, etc.)...
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       else {
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         // It's a two-byte sequence. You might handle it or reset.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         handleSequence(currentSequence);
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         currentState = STATE_IDLE;
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       }
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       break;
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]     case STATE_CSI:
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       currentSequence.push(byte);
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       // Check if this byte is a "final byte" that terminates the CSI sequence
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       if ((byte >= 0x40 && byte <= 0x7E) || byte === 0x7E) { // Letters '@' to '~'
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         // This is the terminator! Sequence is complete.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         handleCsiSequence(currentSequence, params);
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         currentState = STATE_IDLE;
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       } else {
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         // This byte is part of the parameters (digits, semicolons)
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         // Here you would parse the parameters into the `params` array.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]         // For example, bytes 0x31 ( '1' ), 0x39 ( '9' ) become the number 19.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       }
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]       break;
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]     // ... handle other states like STATE_OSC
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat]   }
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 3. Key Insight: The Final Byte
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] The core of your issue is recognizing the terminator. The standard defines the final byte
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] as a byte in the range `0x40-0x7E` (ASCII `@` to `~`). Common terminators you will see:
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • `~` (`0x7E`): Used for function keys (F1-F4, F7-F12, etc.).
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • Letters like `A`, `B`, `C`, `D` (`0x41-0x44`): Used for arrow keys.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • `H` (`0x48`): Home key.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • `R` (`0x52`): Report cursor position (response to `ESC [ 6 n`).
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 4. For Your Specific Example
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] When your parser gets `[27, 91, 49, 56, 126]`:
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 1.  `27` -> moves to `STATE_ESC`.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 2.  `91` ('[') -> moves to `STATE_CSI`.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 3.  `49` ('1') -> parameter data, buffer it.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 4.  `56` ('8') -> parameter data, buffer it.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 5.  `126` ('~') -> Final byte detected! This terminates the sequence. You can now process
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] it as `CSI 18 ~` (F7).
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] Conclusion
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] You are correct—there is no single length or simple `\n` terminator. The rule is: a CSI
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] sequence starts with `ESC [` and ends with a byte in the range `0x40-0x7E`. Building a small
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] state machine is the canonical and most robust way to handle this correctly from a raw byte stream.
2025-08-20 08:32:54 [deepseek-chat] Most terminal libraries (like `node-pty` or `xterm.js`) implement exactly this.
