2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] Immediate likely causes of the issues you mentioned (backspaced tab, pasted emoji showing ???,
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] multiline paste):
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] - decoding one byte at a time:
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini]   - rawPrompt decodes each incoming byte into a single‑byte char (decoder.decode(new
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] Uint8Array([byte]))). UTF‑8 multibyte graphemes (emoji, composed glyphs) will be split and
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] mis‑decoded → “???” or broken characters on paste.
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini]   - Fix approach: decode whole chunk (value) or use TextDecoder with streaming (decode(value,
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] {stream:true})) and append the resulting string; then segment graphemes from that string.
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] - grapheme handling inconsistent:
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini]   - addInput uses a regex to split graphemes in one place and Intl.Segmenter in others (commented).
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] Use one robust approach (Intl.Segmenter) consistently to avoid mismatched widths and backspace
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] behavior.
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] - backspace/tab handling:
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini]   - backspace assumes lastChar is one grapheme and computes width; but when you split bytes
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] incorrectly you may pop a partial grapheme. Use the grapheme array produced by Segmenter so
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] backspace always removes a full grapheme.
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini]   - Tab deletion logic needs to compute tab stops from the current visible width (stringWidth),
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] which is okay — but ensure tabs are stored as '\t' in grapheme array so detection works.
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] - paste / multiline paste:
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini]   - paste often emits a chunk containing many bytes. Your loop treating bytes individually and
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] reacting to escape (0x1b) will mis-handle pasted content that contains bytes matching control
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] codes. Decode the whole incoming buffer, treat it as user input (not command) unless it starts with
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini] explicit ESC sequences.
2025-08-20 12:17:06 [gpt-5-mini]   - Consider a short “paste mode”: if the chunk length is large (> X bytes) treat it as paste and
