2025-08-20 12:29:48 [nitro@ryzen5] are there any more formal cite methods available?
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] Thank you for your question! Yes, several formal citation standards can be applied to songs and
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] bibliographic entries like those in your biblispec JSON. These provide structured ways to reference
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] audio recordings, lyrics, or performances. Here's a brief overview of common methods, with examples
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] tailored to "Whitey on the Moon" for illustration:
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] 1. MLA (Modern Language Association)
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Focus: Humanities; emphasizes author and title.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Example: Scott-Heron, Gil. "Whitey on the Moon." Small Talk at 125th and Lenox, Flying
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] Dutchman Records, 1970.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Usage: Integrate as a string in JSON under a "citation" > "mla" field.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] 2. APA (American Psychological Association)
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Focus: Social sciences; prioritizes author, date, and source.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Example: Scott-Heron, G. (1970). Whitey on the moon [Song]. On Small Talk at 125th and
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] Lenox. Flying Dutchman Records.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Usage: Good for academic papers; add to JSON as "citation" > "apa".
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] 3. Chicago/Turabian
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Focus: History and arts; offers note-bibliography or author-date styles.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Example (Note): Gil Scott-Heron, "Whitey on the Moon," track 1 on Small Talk at 125th and
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] Lenox, Flying Dutchman Records, LP, 1970.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Usage: Flexible for footnotes; store in JSON as "citation" > "chicago".
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] 4. Harvard
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Focus: Author-date system, common in UK academia.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Example: Scott-Heron, G. (1970) 'Whitey on the moon', in Small Talk at 125th and
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] Lenox, Flying Dutchman Records.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Usage: Simple inline citations.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] 5. BibTeX (for LaTeX)
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Focus: Technical/scientific writing; machine-readable.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Example:
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]      @misc{scottheron1970whitey,
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]        author = {Scott-Heron, Gil},
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]        title = {Whitey on the Moon},
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]        year = {1970},
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]        howpublished = {From \emph{Small Talk at 125th and Lenox} [Album]. Flying Dutchman Records}
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]      }
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Usage: Ideal for exporting to tools like Overleaf; embed as a raw string in JSON.
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709] 6. RIS (Research Information Systems)
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Focus: Interchange format for reference managers (e.g., Zotero, EndNote).
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Example:
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]      TY  - SOUN
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]      TI  - Whitey on the Moon
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]      AU  - Scott-Heron, Gil
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]      PY  - 1970
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]      PB  - Flying Dutchman Records
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]      AL  - Small Talk at 125th and Lenox
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]      ER  -
2025-08-20 12:30:07 [grok-4-0709]    - Usage: Easy import/export; store as multiline text in JSON.
