2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] No, the timer (setInterval) does not interrupt the `await reader.read()` command. Here's a brief
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] explanation:
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] - `await reader.read()` is an asynchronous operation that pauses execution of the `rawPrompt`
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] function until the read promise resolves (e.g., when input is available from the stream).
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] - The timer runs independently in the event loop. Its callbacks (e.g., the refreshHandler) will
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] only execute when the loop is idle, such as between awaits in your loop, but not midway
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] through a pending `read()` promise.
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] - This means the timer can "run concurrently" but won't preempt or cancel the read operation. If
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] the read takes a long time (e.g., waiting for input), the timer might queue up multiple callbacks.
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] If the timer is causing unexpected behavior (e.g., due to concurrency issues with shared state like
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] `grapheme`), consider clearing it with `clearInterval(timer)` when appropriate (e.g., after the
2025-08-20 16:34:38 [grok-4-0709] loop breaks). If you'd like code suggestions or fixes for this snippet, provide more details!
