2025-08-20 16:43:25 [nitro@ryzen5] what is nicest way to call reader.read with a timeout?
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] Hello! In Deno, to call `reader.read()` with a timeout on a `ReadableStream` (like from
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] `Deno.stdin`), a clean approach is to use `Promise.race()` to compete against a timeout Promise.
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] This lets you poll or refresh background tasks periodically if no input arrives.
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] Here's a high-level example structure (pseudocode for clarity):
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] - Create a timeout Promise: `new Promise((_, reject) => setTimeout(() => reject(new
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] Error('Timeout')), ms))`.
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] - Race it: `Promise.race([reader.read(), timeoutPromise])`.
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] - If timeout wins, handle your refresh logic and loop back to try reading again.
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] This avoids blocking and allows servicing background tasks. Be mindful that if timeout occurs, the
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] read Promise might still be pending, so you may need an `AbortController` for cancellation if the
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] stream supports it.
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] If you'd like a code snippet or more details tailored to your `rawPrompt2` function, just let me
2025-08-20 16:43:35 [grok-4-0709] know!
