2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] This sounds like you're encountering a common issue with the carriage return (`\r`, ASCII 13) and
2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] line feed (`\n`, ASCII 10) characters in a Windows environment.
2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] On Windows, a proper newline is represented by the sequence `\r\n` (CRLF). If your application or
2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] script is only sending `\n` (LF), the cursor will return to the start of the next line
2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] instead of the start of the current line, which can cause the behavior you're describing
2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] where text overwrites the previous line.
2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] • To fix this, ensure you are using the correct `\r\n` sequence for newlines on Windows.*
2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] If you are generating the text, please replace standalone `\n` characters with `\r\n`. If you are
2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] working with code, here is a generic example of ensuring correct line endings:
2025-08-21 15:20:14 [deepseek-chat] // Example: Convert Unix-style line endings to Windows-style
