2025-08-21 15:30:14 [nitro@ryzen5] there is one quirk on windows where once text is at bottom of screen cursor moves up one line after and enter key
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] This is a classic Windows terminal quirk related to how the console handles line wrapping and
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] cursor positioning. The issue occurs because:
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] Root Cause
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] Windows terminals handle line wrapping differently than Unix-style terminals. When text reaches the
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] bottom:
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] 1. The terminal automatically scrolls up one line
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] 2. But the cursor positioning logic doesn't always account for this scroll
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] 3. After Enter, the cursor ends up one line higher than expected
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] Potential Solutions
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] • 1. Manual Cursor Adjustment*
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] // After Enter is pressed, add cursor adjustment
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] bytes.push(0x0D, 0x0A);
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] // Add explicit cursor down movement for Windows
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] if (Deno.build.os === "windows") {
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat]     bytes.push(0x1B, 0x5B, 0x42); // ESC [ B (cursor down)
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] • 2. Force Cursor Repositioning*
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] // After processing input, explicitly move to correct position
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] const currentLine = Math.floor(cursorPosition / terminalWidth);
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] const newPosition = (currentLine + 1) * terminalWidth;
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] await writer.write(encoder.encode(`\x1B[${newPosition}H`));
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] • 3. Use Windows-specific ANSI sequences*
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] // Windows may need explicit scroll region commands
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] if (Deno.build.os === "windows") {
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat]     bytes.push(0x1B, 0x5B, 0x72); // ESC [ r (reset scroll region)
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] The most reliable approach would be to detect Windows OS and add compensatory cursor movements
2025-08-21 15:30:36 [deepseek-chat] after line wrapping occurs.
2025-08-21 15:32:13 [nitro@ryzen5] what order, is it 13 10 or 10 13 , what ansi key codes do i get
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] On Windows, the correct order is CR then LF (13 then 10).
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] Enter Key Sequence
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] - Windows: `\r\n` (CRLF) → `[13, 10]`
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] - Unix: `\n` (LF) → `[10]`
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] ANSI Key Codes for Enter
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] // Windows Enter key produces:
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] const enterSequence = [13, 10]; // CR, LF
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] // Unix/Linux/Mac Enter produces:
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] const enterSequence = [10]; // LF only
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] Common ANSI Sequences
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] - Cursor Up: `[27, 91, 65]` (ESC [ A)
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] - Cursor Down: `[27, 91, 66]` (ESC [ B)
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] - Cursor Right: `[27, 91, 67]` (ESC [ C)
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] - Cursor Left: `[27, 91, 68]` (ESC [ D)
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] - Home: `[27, 91, 72]` (ESC [ H)
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] - End: `[27, 91, 70]` (ESC [ F)
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] For the Windows cursor issue, you'll want to handle the `[13, 10]` sequence and potentially add
2025-08-21 15:32:34 [deepseek-chat] compensatory cursor movement after processing it.
