2025-08-21 18:29:26 [nitro@ryzen5] ❤️without an feof test
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] It's important to always test for EOF (end-of-file) when reading from files to avoid infinite loops
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] and handle file reading properly. Without an EOF test, your program may attempt to read past the
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] end of the file, which can cause errors or unexpected behavior.
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] Here's a proper example using `feof()`:
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] FILE *file = fopen("example.txt", "r");
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] if (file == NULL) {
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat]     perror("Error opening file");
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat]     return 1;
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] char buffer[256];
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] while (!feof(file)) {
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat]     if (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), file) != NULL) {
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat]         printf("%s", buffer);
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat]     }
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-08-21 18:29:39 [deepseek-chat] fclose(file);
