2025-08-21 18:56:52 [nitro@ryzen5] what byte should I write to move cursor right?
2025-08-21 18:57:05 [deepseek-chat] For moving the cursor right in a terminal, you typically write the ASCII byte for the right arrow
2025-08-21 18:57:05 [deepseek-chat] key. However, arrow keys are often represented by escape sequences rather than a single byte.
2025-08-21 18:57:05 [deepseek-chat] On most Unix-like systems, the right arrow key sends the escape sequence: `\x1b[C` (which is 3
2025-08-21 18:57:05 [deepseek-chat] bytes: ESC, [, C).
2025-08-21 18:57:05 [deepseek-chat] If you specifically need a single byte, standard ASCII control characters like:
2025-08-21 18:57:05 [deepseek-chat] - `0x09` (tab) moves cursor to next tab stop
2025-08-21 18:57:05 [deepseek-chat] - `0x20` (space) moves cursor right one position
2025-08-21 18:57:05 [deepseek-chat] But for precise cursor control, escape sequences are standard.
2025-08-21 18:58:21 [nitro@ryzen5] 	bytes.push(0x1b,'[','C');
2025-08-21 18:58:28 [deepseek-chat] That's correct. This sequence (ESC, '[', 'C') is the standard ANSI escape code for moving the
2025-08-21 18:58:28 [deepseek-chat] cursor one position to the right.
