2025-08-21 19:37:34 [nitro@ryzen5] F8 key is available for copy block, does deno support clipboard
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] Yes—Deno can read and write the system clipboard, but only when the process is started with the
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] `--allow-read` (and on some OS’s `--allow-write`) flags, because most clipboard APIs are
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] considered sensitive.
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] Deno ≥ 1.35 includes the built-in API:
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] // copy
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] await navigator.clipboard.writeText("copied text");
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] // paste
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] const txt = await navigator.clipboard.readText();
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] Works on:
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] - Windows 10/11 – full support
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] - macOS – full support
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] - Linux / WSL – you must have `xclip`, `xsel`, or `wl-clipboard` installed; Deno will use
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] the first one found.
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] If you need polyfills or prefer the shell path, `slopfountain.ts` already has a `clipText()` helper
2025-08-21 19:38:06 [kimi-k2-0711] that wraps `pbcopy`, `clip`, or `xclip`.
