2025-08-21 19:47:49 [nitro@ryzen5] so when i add an emoji code point i should always add some kind of terminator?
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] Good question! For emoji code points, it depends on the context:
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] • For text rendering/display:* No terminator needed. Emoji code points stand alone (e.g.,
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] `U+1F600` for 😀).
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] • For string processing/storage:* Generally no terminator, but consider:
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] - Use `\uFE0F` (Variation Selector-16) after emoji to ensure emoji-style rendering (e.g., `❤️` vs
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] `❤`)
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] - Some emoji are sequences (like skin tones: 👋 + 🏼 = 👋🏼)
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] • In your shortcode system:* Your current implementation handles this correctly by
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] appending `\uFE0F` (as seen in `sloprawprompt.ts`), which ensures consistent emoji presentation.
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] So yes, for display consistency, appending `\uFE0F` is a good practice, but it's not always
2025-08-21 19:48:10 [deepseek-chat] strictly necessary for the emoji to function. Your implementation already does this properly! 👍
