2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] Good — here's a short, practical plan to sanitize bytes before writing to Windows stdio so you
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] avoid InvalidData.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] Principles
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - If data is text or ANSI: treat it as text. Build it as strings and use TextEncoder.encode(...) so
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] bytes are valid UTF‑8.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - If you must accept arbitrary bytes, replace any invalid UTF‑8 sequences with the Unicode
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] replacement character (U+FFFD) encoded as UTF‑8 (0xEF 0xBF 0xBD) before writing.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - Preserve intended control/ANSI escapes where possible by recognizing and keeping valid escape
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] sequences, but still ensure surrounding bytes form valid UTF‑8.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] Sanitization strategy (high level)
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] 1) Decide intent:
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]    - Text/ANSI -> encode from string.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]    - Binary -> do not write to console; write to file or base64 for display.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] 2) UTF‑8 validation + replacement:
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]    - Scan the input byte buffer sequentially.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]    - For each byte:
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]      - If 0x00..0x7F: ASCII byte — append as-is.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]      - If leading byte indicates multi‑byte sequence (0xC0..0xF7): determine expected continuation
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] length (1..3), verify that the following bytes are in 0x80..0xBF.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]        - If valid, append the whole sequence.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]        - If invalid (missing or bad continuation), append UTF‑8 for U+FFFD and advance by one (or
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] skip the invalid continuation bytes).
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]      - If byte is a continuation byte (0x80..0xBF) encountered alone, treat it as invalid -> append
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] U+FFFD and skip.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]      - If byte is >0xF7 or otherwise invalid, append U+FFFD.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]    - The result is a new byte buffer that is valid UTF‑8.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] 3) Handle ANSI / control escapes (optional refinement):
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]    - If you want to allow ESC-based ANSI sequences (ESC '[' ... letters), detect sequences starting
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] with 0x1B and validate they match common ANSI CSI patterns; copy them through if valid; otherwise
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] treat ESC alone as valid ASCII 0x1B or replace with safe representation.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]    - But still perform UTF‑8 validation for the rest.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] 4) Re-encode and write:
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]    - If you reconstructed sanitized bytes directly, call writer.write(sanitizedUint8Array).
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini]    - Or decode sanitized bytes to string (TextDecoder without fatal) and then TextEncoder.encode to
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] get canonical UTF‑8 bytes; write that.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] Diagnostics
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - When you encounter invalid bytes, log a short hex snapshot (first N invalid bytes) so you can
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] trace the source (paste, binary data, split grapheme).
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - Optionally count and report how many replacements were made.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] Performance notes
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - Do this in a single pass; avoid building many intermediate arrays.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - Pre-allocate an output Uint8Array if you can estimate size; otherwise push into a small
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] Array<number> and convert once.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - For heavy throughput, prefer building strings and encoding once; repeated per-byte work is
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] slower.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] When to convert instead of sanitize
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - If content originates as user text (shortcodes, emoji), better to convert to string early and
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] encode — avoids per-byte repairs.
2025-08-22 09:58:21 [gpt-5-mini] - If content is truly binary, do not send to console; write to file or base64 it for display.
